In-Reply-To: <pszEX4ZIxLk.mms8efkWA0IJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <pszEX4ZIxLk.mms8efkWA0IJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/pszEX4ZIxLk/m/Ew8jjMYcvvoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

New option added to allow for dropping specific factors internally by using NA's in place of numeric input. Update is on github and ?bfactor contains a new example specifically for this.

On Sunday, April 21, 2013 7:17:57 PM UTC-4, Phil Chalmers wrote:
>
> Email question:
>
>
> Using your package ‘mirt’, is it possible to do a bifactor analysis where some of the items only load on the general factor and on none of the specific factors?
>
>
> Reply:
>
>
> Yes that it possible, though you'll be required to declare an arbitrary specific vector anyway and modify the internal elements manually. For example, use
>
> ######
> sv <- bfactor(data, specific, pars = 'values')
> sv #modify the elements you want in here, changing their values and changing est to FALSE
> sv$value[2] <-  0
> sv$est[2] <-  FALSE
> mod <- bfactor(data, specific, pars = sv)
> #####
>
> Hope that helps.
>

